 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Polarity",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Pin 1 is the anode (+) and pin 2 the cathode. On one face a green corner mark sits at the pin 2 end; on the other a green triangle points toward pin 2. In the carrier tape the anode faces away from the sprocket holes and the cathode toward them."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Drive",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Run the LED at its rated current with a current-limiting resistor in the circuit: without one, a small change in supply voltage moves the current a lot and can destroy it. Keep switching surges off it, and never put more than 5 V of reverse voltage across it, in use or in test. Too high a forward or reverse voltage damages it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current, temperature and colour",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "The emitted colour shifts a little with drive current, and a resistor in series with each LED is the suggested arrangement. Self-heating and ambient temperature change it too: a hotter LED is less efficient and its colour moves, so heat removal belongs in the design. Above 25 °C ambient the allowed DC current falls from 25 mA to about 7 mA at 85 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bins",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Build one product from a single bin; mixing bins can give visible colour differences between LEDs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Handling and soldering",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The encapsulant is soft, so pressure on the emitting face or the plastic body damages it; pick it up gently. Reflow it to the profile with an easy-clean flux, two reflows at most, and keep the emitting face free of residue, which alters the colour. Hand soldering is for repair and rework only: one pass, the tip no hotter than 300 °C, done within 3 s, with an iron of 25 W at most. Keep tools and fingers off the hot lens, and once soldered keep force, shock and vibration off it and do not flex the board."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Cleaning and chemicals",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "No ultrasonic cleaning. Wipe with, or dip in, isopropyl or pure alcohol for no more than one minute, then leave 15 minutes at room temperature before use. Thinners, trichloroethylene, acetone, sulphides, nitrides, acids, alkalis and salts attack the LED."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Potting and adhesives",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Volatile compounds can get into the package and, under light and heat in operation, discolour it and cut its output sharply. Test every potting or bonding material for compatibility, avoid adhesives that release organic vapour, and with an ordinary potting compound first run a small batch lit for 168 h in a room-temperature trial."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Storage and moisture",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Sealed, keep at 30 °C or below and under 60 % RH, and use within a year. The part is moisture sensitive: once the bag is opened, use it within 7 days at 30 °C or below and within 60 % RH, or it may crack in reflow. Dry parts left over by baking, 60 ±5 °C for 24 h on the reel or 105 ±5 °C for 1 h loose, then reseal them in a foil bag or keep them in a nitrogen dry cabinet. Keep acids, alkalis, corrosive gas, heavy vibration and strong magnetic fields away."
  },
  {
   "topic": "ESD",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Static and voltage spikes can kill it, including the transient at switch-on or switch-off. Handle with grounded wrist straps, gloves and shoes, and ground the tools and instruments. A damaged part shows much more leakage, a lower forward voltage at small currents, or no light at small currents."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Packing",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "4000 pieces to a reel on 8 mm carrier tape. The reel carries a label and is sealed in an anti-static, moisture-proof aluminium foil bag."
  }
